[Id "kings-indian"]
[Name "King's Indian Defense"]
[Side "black"]
[ECO "E60-E99"]
[Description "Let White take the centre, then strike back with …e5 and a kingside attack."]
[Tags "aggressive"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4
  (2. Bf4 {@variation vs the London} 2... g6 {…g6: the same King's Indian set-up works against the London.} 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Be2 d6 {…d6 prepares …e5 or …c5 against White's quiet set-up.} 6. h3 c5 {…c5 hits d4 and opens the long diagonal for the g7 bishop.}
{@line id=kings-indian-vs-london level=1 tier=side name="vs the London (2.Bf4)" summary="Against the London, keep the fianchetto and strike with …c5 at d4."}
)
2... g6 {…g6 prepares …Bg7, a bishop that will press on the long diagonal.} 3. Nc3
  (3. Nf3 {@variation Fianchetto} 3... Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 {…Nbd7 supports …e5 against White's solid fianchetto.} 7. Nc3 e5 {…e5 claims the centre.} 8. e4 c6 {…c6 controls d5 and prepares …Qb6 or …a5.}
{@line id=kings-indian-fianchetto level=2 tier=side name="Fianchetto (g3)" summary="Against the fianchetto, play …Nbd7, …e5 and …c6: a solid centre with ideas of …Qb6."}
)
3... Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 {@variation Classical}
  (5. f3 {@variation Samisch} 5... O-O {…O-O first; White's f3 means a kingside pawn storm may come.} 6. Be3 e5 {…e5 hits the centre, the same answer as against the Classical.} 7. d5 Nh5 {…Nh5 prepares …f5 and eyes the f4 square.} 8. Qd2 f5 {…f5 strikes at e4, Black's main lever in the King's Indian.}
{@line id=kings-indian-samisch level=1 tier=main name="Samisch (5.f3)" summary="Against f3 and Be3, play …e5, then …Nh5 and …f5 to break up White's pawn centre."}
)
  (5. f4 {@variation Four Pawns Attack} 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c5 {…c5 attacks the overextended centre right away.} 7. d5 e6 {…e6 breaks open the e-file against White's uncastled king.} 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 Bg4 {…Bg4 pins the f3 knight, which supports White's e5 push.}
{@line id=kings-indian-four-pawns level=2 tier=side name="Four Pawns Attack (5.f4)" summary="The big pawn centre is a target: hit it with …c5 and …e6, then pin the f3 knight."}
)
5... O-O 6. Be2 e5 {…e5 is Black's central strike; it fixes the centre for the plans to come.} 7. O-O
  (7. dxe5 dxe5 {…dxe5 recaptures; the queens come off but Black is fine.} 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 {…Rxd8 takes the open file; the d4 square is a good outpost for a black knight.}
{@line id=kings-indian-exchange level=1 tier=side name="Exchange (7.dxe5)" summary="The early queen trade isn't scary: take with the rook and aim a knight at d4."}
)
  (7. d5 a5 {…a5 slows White's b4 push on the queenside.} 8. Bg5 h6 {…h6 asks the bishop to decide; it can't keep the pin forever.} 9. Bh4 Na6 {…Na6 heads for c5, a great square for the knight.} 10. Nd2 Qe8 {…Qe8 unpins the f6 knight so Black can play …Nh7 and …f5.}
{@line id=kings-indian-petrosian level=2 tier=main name="Petrosian (7.d5)" summary="Against an early d5, stop b4 with …a5, then knight to c5 and break the pin before …f5."}
)
7... Nc6 {…Nc6 adds pressure on d4 and invites d5.} 8. d5 Ne7 {…Ne7 heads to the kingside, where Black plays …f5 next.}
{@line id=kings-indian-classical level=1 tier=main name="8.d5 Ne7" summary="Fianchetto, castle, strike with …e5; when White locks the centre with d5, attack with …f5."}
9. Ne1
  (9. b4 Nh5 {…Nh5 prepares …f5 at once, faster than …Nd7.} 10. Re1 f5 {…f5 hits e4 as usual.} 11. Ng5 Nf6 {…Nf6 brings the knight back to the centre and covers h7 against the g5 knight.}
{@line id=kings-indian-bayonet level=2 tier=side name="Bayonet (9.b4)" summary="Against b4, go for …Nh5 and …f5 straight away; speed matters in the race."}
)
9... Nd7 {…Nd7 clears the f-pawn for …f5.} 10. Nd3 f5 {…f5 opens the kingside fight; White attacks on the queenside.} 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 {…f4 gains space; next come …g5-g4 and a direct attack on White's king.}
{@line id=kings-indian-mar-del-plata level=2 tier=main name="Mar del Plata (9.Ne1)" summary="The race: Black storms the kingside with …f5, …f4, …g5; White pushes c5 on the queenside."} *
